.Dd April 13, 2026
.Dt EFIDEV 4D
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm efidev
.Nd UEFI variable access pseudo-device driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/efiio.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/efi
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides
.Pa /dev/efi ,
a character device that exposes UEFI Runtime Services variable operations
to userland.
It is intended as the kernel interface for tools such as
.Nm efibootmgr
and
.Nm efivar .
.Pp
Three ioctls are provided:
.Bl -tag -width EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET
Read a variable by name and vendor GUID.
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET
Create, modify, or delete a variable.
Requires the
.Sy PRIV_SYS_CONFIG
privilege.
Setting
.Fa vi_data_size
to 0 deletes the variable.
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
Enumerate variable names.
On the first call, seed the name buffer with an empty
.Pq NUL-only
UCS-2 string.
On subsequent calls, seed with the name returned by the previous call.
.El
.Ss Data Structure
All three ioctls use a pointer to an
.Vt efi_var_ioc_t
structure, defined in
.In sys/efiio.h :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct efi_var_ioc {
	uint16_t	*vi_name;
	size_t		vi_name_size;
	efi_guid_t	vi_vendor;
	uint32_t	vi_attrib;
	void		*vi_data;
	size_t		vi_data_size;
} efi_var_ioc_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa vi_name
points to a UCS-2
.Pq 16-bit
encoded, NUL-terminated variable name buffer.
.Fa vi_name_size
and
.Fa vi_data_size
are in/out fields expressed in bytes: on entry they describe the buffer
sizes; on return they reflect the actual sizes.
.Fa vi_vendor
is the variable's namespace GUID.
.Fa vi_attrib
contains the EFI variable attribute bitmask
.Pq returned by Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET , supplied for Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET .
.Ss Buffer Size Discovery
If the caller-supplied buffer is too small,
.Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET
and
.Dv EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
update the size field in the
.Vt efi_var_ioc_t
structure and return
.Er EOVERFLOW .
The caller can then retry with an appropriately sized buffer.
.Ss Limits
Variable names are limited to 1024 bytes and variable data to 32768
bytes.
These are limits imposed by the driver; the UEFI specification
does not mandate maximum sizes.
.Sh IOCTLS
.Bl -tag -width EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET
.Pp
Input:
.Fa vi_name ,
.Fa vi_name_size ,
.Fa vi_vendor ,
.Fa vi_data ,
.Fa vi_data_size .
.Pp
Output:
.Fa vi_data
.Pq filled ,
.Fa vi_data_size
.Pq actual ,
.Fa vi_attrib .
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET
.Pp
Input:
.Fa vi_name ,
.Fa vi_name_size ,
.Fa vi_vendor ,
.Fa vi_attrib ,
.Fa vi_data ,
.Fa vi_data_size .
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
.Pp
Input:
.Fa vi_name
.Pq previous name or empty ,
.Fa vi_name_size ,
.Fa vi_vendor .
.Pp
Output:
.Fa vi_name
.Pq next name ,
.Fa vi_name_size
.Pq actual ,
.Fa vi_vendor .
.El
.Sh ERRORS
The ioctls may return the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EINVAL
An argument was invalid
.Pq NULL name, odd name size, size exceeds limits, unterminated UCS-2 name .
.It Er ENOENT
The variable was not found
.Pq Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET
or there are no more variables
.Pq Dv EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT .
.It Er EOVERFLOW
The supplied buffer was too small; the required size has been written
back to the
.Vt efi_var_ioc_t
structure.
.It Er EPERM
.Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET
was attempted without the
.Sy PRIV_SYS_CONFIG
privilege.
.It Er EACCES
Firmware rejected the operation due to a security policy violation.
.It Er EFAULT
A bad address was supplied.
.It Er EIO
Firmware returned an unexpected error.
.It Er ENOTSUP
The requested runtime service is not supported by firmware or has previously
faulted and is now unavailable.
.It Er EROFS
The variable is write-protected.
.It Er ENXIO
UEFI Runtime Services are not available on this system.
.El
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
Volatile
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr privileges 7
.Pp
.Rs
.%T Unified Extensible Firmware Interface Specification
.%N Version 2.10
.%O Section 8.2
.Re
.Sh NOTES
The
.Pa /dev/efi
device is only present on systems with UEFI firmware that provides
Runtime Services.
Not all UEFI implementations support all variable operations.
.Pp
Variable names use UCS-2 encoding
.Pq 16-bit characters
but sizes are specified in bytes.
